/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  membrane20_dspmde_pore_finite_difference  --  the charged pore SOLVED, and
  the wall closed with the permeate it gives, or with one it does not.

  FOUR NF270 elements, two salts -- every unit has the SAME membrane
  (NF270_dspmde: r_p 0.6 nm, X_d = -5 mol/m3, Born on), the same 10 bar, the
  same 5 mM feed of its salt, and the same SOLVED pore (101 nodes):

       NF_NaCl_pore      NF_MgSO4_pore     wallClosure porePermeate;
       NF_NaCl_BsWall    NF_MgSO4_BsWall   wallClosure solutionDiffusion;

  The `_pore` units DECLARE what has been DSPM-DE's default since
  2026-10-24 (DEV.md 4c C141, C142) -- the pore solved, the wall closed with
  that pore's own permeate -- so the declaration is visible; membrane10 and
  membrane11 run the same model with nothing declared.

  WHAT THE SOLVED PORE IS.  The extended Nernst-Planck equation of every ion
  on 101 nodes across the pore, electroneutrality with the fixed charge at
  EVERY node, zero net current, and the Donnan + steric + Born partitions at
  both faces; and, in the SAME Newton (C142), the film's interface balance at
  the wall and the water flux J_w = A_w (dP - dpi) -- one Newton per station
  on the declared block-tridiagonal structure, with an analytic Jacobian.
  The problem is STIFF -- a co-ion held back by the Donnan field carries a
  migration and a diffusion flux far larger than the flux they net, and the
  run prints that ratio ("stiffness") beside its residuals.  NanoFiltran
  (Geraldes & Alves, "Computer program for simulation of mass transport in
  nanofiltration membranes", J. Membr. Sci. 321 (2008) 172-182) is the
  reference it is compared with, in tutorials/props/membrane/
  nanofiltran_geraldesAlves2008 (the paper's one case).

  WHAT THE PAIR TEACHES: THE WALL MUST BE CLOSED WITH THE PERMEATE THE
  MEMBRANE MAKES.  The film's interface balance (Geraldes & Afonso, J.
  Membr. Sci. 300 (2007) 20, Eq. 1) says the solute leaving the wall into the
  membrane is J_v c_p.  `porePermeate` puts there the permeate THIS pore
  makes, so the film delivers to the wall exactly what the pore carries away
  -- solute is conserved across the interface.  `solutionDiffusion` puts
  there c_p = B_s c_m/(J_v + B_s) with the record's solution-diffusion B_s --
  a permeate from ANOTHER model, which the pore does not make -- so the film
  is solved for a solute flux that does not cross the membrane.  (It is the
  wall the retired closed form had, and C138 traced a NEGATIVE NaCl
  rejection on membrane11 to it.)  The gap measures how far that B_s is from
  what the pore transmits:
    NaCl   B_s 5e-6 m/s says c_p ~ 0.06 c_m (94 % at the wall); the pore
           transmits about 0.16 c_m (its R_obs 49.7 % over a wall 3.07
           times the bulk), nearly three times more.  The B_s wall
           believes almost all the salt is held back, so its film must
           carry more salt back to the bulk and over-polarises (Gamma 2.79
           against 2.07); the pore is fed that richer wall, and R_obs falls
           from 49.73 % to 30.80 %.
    MgSO4  both permeates are a few parts in ten thousand of the wall, the
           film carries back essentially everything either way, and the two
           R_obs agree to 1e-6 (99.9751 % / 99.9752 %).
  So the closure is a modelling decision exactly where the permeate is not
  negligible -- which, in nanofiltration, is the monovalent salt.

  THE RETIRED CLOSED FORM.  Until 2026-10-24 this case carried a
  `method closedForm;` unit (the default until C141): the pore made
  electroneutral at the feed face only, each ion carried across ALONE by a
  closed-form transmission with no field coupling the ions inside.  With the
  corrected hindrance (K_d = H/Phi, C141) that transmission tends to K_c > 1
  at low Peclet number -- it put more NaCl in the permeate than the wall
  held and exhausted the bulk, and gave MgSO4 58 % where the solved pore
  gives 99.98 %.  It is RETIRED (DEV.md 4c C142): the word refuses by name
  with that reason.

  WHAT TO LOOK AT.  The four R_obs and Gamma, and the [DSPM-DE] lines: the
  node count, the first station's Newton iterations and residual, the
  block-tridiagonal structure and the analytic Jacobian VERIFIED on the
  first solve, the pore summary per unit (flux constancy, zero current and
  electroneutrality, each measured on the answer), and the continuation the
  MgSO4 units need at their first station (announced and counted).

  WHAT THIS CASE DOES NOT CLAIM.  Nothing in it is validated against any
  measurement.  The grid, the residuals, the analytic Jacobian and the
  analytic limit are held by bin/curate/check_pore_solver.py.  Shared by all
  four units: the Davies activity at the feed face, an ideal permeate,
  van't Hoff osmotic pressure.

  Data: as membrane10_dspmde_divalent -- the salts carry their ion
  decomposition, the membrane its poreModel{} tier, the ions their radius
  and D0 from the species records.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        NF_NaCl_pore;
        type        spiralWoundModule;
        in          feed_NaCl_pore;
        outputs     ( ret_NaCl_pore  perm_NaCl_pore );
        operation
        {
            membrane         NF270_dspmde;
            transport        DSPM-DE;
            transportParameters
            {
                poreSolver { method finiteDifference;  nodes 101;  wallClosure porePermeate; }
            }
            area             5.0;
            length           1.0;
            P_permeate       1.0 bar;
            k_film           5.0e-5;
            dP_feed_total    1.0 bar;
            nNodes           20;
        }
    }
    {
        name        NF_NaCl_BsWall;
        type        spiralWoundModule;
        in          feed_NaCl_BsWall;
        outputs     ( ret_NaCl_BsWall  perm_NaCl_BsWall );
        operation
        {
            membrane         NF270_dspmde;
            transport        DSPM-DE;
            transportParameters
            {
                poreSolver { method finiteDifference;  nodes 101;  wallClosure solutionDiffusion; }
            }
            area             5.0;
            length           1.0;
            P_permeate       1.0 bar;
            k_film           5.0e-5;
            dP_feed_total    1.0 bar;
            nNodes           20;
        }
    }
    {
        name        NF_MgSO4_pore;
        type        spiralWoundModule;
        in          feed_MgSO4_pore;
        outputs     ( ret_MgSO4_pore  perm_MgSO4_pore );
        operation
        {
            membrane         NF270_dspmde;
            transport        DSPM-DE;
            transportParameters
            {
                poreSolver { method finiteDifference;  nodes 101;  wallClosure porePermeate; }
            }
            area             5.0;
            length           1.0;
            P_permeate       1.0 bar;
            k_film           5.0e-5;
            dP_feed_total    1.0 bar;
            nNodes           20;
        }
    }
    {
        name        NF_MgSO4_BsWall;
        type        spiralWoundModule;
        in          feed_MgSO4_BsWall;
        outputs     ( ret_MgSO4_BsWall  perm_MgSO4_BsWall );
        operation
        {
            membrane         NF270_dspmde;
            transport        DSPM-DE;
            transportParameters
            {
                poreSolver { method finiteDifference;  nodes 101;  wallClosure solutionDiffusion; }
            }
            area             5.0;
            length           1.0;
            P_permeate       1.0 bar;
            k_film           5.0e-5;
            dP_feed_total    1.0 bar;
            nNodes           20;
        }
    }
);
